cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file, and cite the source
  paper listed under "references". This is a draft reference implementation: see
  experiments/results/RESULTS.md for the values it produces and the conditions under which it
  produces them.
title: "ddqn-os-scheduling: a reference implementation of Double DQN operating-system scheduling with guardrails"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Guo
    given-names: Fan
repository-code: "https://github.com/fguo10-tech/ddqn-os-scheduling"
version: 0.1.0.dev0
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  Draft, reproducible reference implementation of Double DQN operating-system scheduling with
  guardrails, for researchers who want to run, benchmark and extend it. It provides a
  discrete-event single-CPU operating-system scheduling simulator with synthetic workloads at
  three load levels, FCFS, SJF, Round Robin and Priority baselines, a preemptive
  shortest-remaining-time-first reference, a Double DQN agent whose reward penalises waiting,
  turnaround, slow response and deadline misses, three guardrails (starvation override, SLO
  fallback to Round Robin and a Q-divergence latch), and a SchedulerPlugin interface with
  job-queue adapter sketches. Task classes are defined in configuration, and a second, synthetic
  workload profile shaped like the work of an LLM platform (inference requests and fine-tuning
  jobs) is reported with per-class metrics; no language model is run. Results are reported as a
  Before / After comparison under documented conditions. Every number in the documentation is
  labelled Paper-reported, Reproduced or Target / TBD.
keywords:
  - reinforcement learning
  - Double DQN
  - operating system scheduling
  - discrete-event simulation
  - guardrails
  - workload profiles
  - reproducibility
references:
  - type: conference-paper
    title: "Dynamic Operating System Scheduling Using Double DQN: A Reinforcement Learning Approach to Task Optimization"
